{%- comment -%}
  "DRINKS LIKE …" — one construction, everywhere.

  THE BUG THIS FIXES. custom.sits holds a fragment on every bottle:

      NON1  "A dry rosé sat"        NON5  "A dry sparkling sat"
      NON2  "A rich white sat"      NON7  "A big red sat"
      NON3  "An aromatic white sat" NON9  "A pinot noir sat"

  It was written to finish a sentence the page never says — something like
  "…where a dry rosé sat" — and it was being printed on its own, as the note
  under every product card and as a spec-strip value. "A dry rosé sat ·$30.00"
  is not a sentence, and a customer reads it as a typo before they read it as a
  description.

  WHAT IT SAYS INSTEAD. The trailing verb is dropped and the phrase is turned
  into a complete statement about the drinking experience:

      "Drinks like a dry rosé."

  "Drinks like" is doing careful work. It describes how the bottle behaves in
  the glass and at the table without claiming the bottle IS wine — which it is
  not, and which is the one claim this brand must never make. "Tastes like a
  rosé" is an imitation claim; "drinks like a dry rosé" is a description of
  weight, structure and where it sits in a meal.

  PREFERRED SOURCE FIRST. custom.drinks_like, when it exists, is used verbatim
  — that is where a properly written phrase ("a dry, savoury rosé") belongs,
  and it lets the copy be improved per bottle without touching the theme. The
  derivation below is the fallback, so every bottle reads as a sentence today
  whether or not anyone has written the better version yet.

  Usage:
    {% render 'drinks-like', product: product %}                → full sentence
    {% render 'drinks-like', product: product, bare: true %}    → phrase only
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign explicit = product.metafields.custom.drinks_like | metafield_text | strip
  assign sits = product.metafields.custom.sits | metafield_text | strip

  assign phrase = ''

  if explicit != blank
    assign phrase = explicit
  elsif sits != blank
    # Strip the dangling verb. Only ever at the END, and only as a whole word,
    # so a style that legitimately contains the letters is untouched.
    assign phrase = sits
    if phrase contains ' sat'
      assign parts = phrase | split: ' sat'
      assign phrase = parts | first | strip
    endif
    # Lowercase the leading article so it reads inside a sentence rather than
    # starting a new one. Done by hand: Liquid's downcase would flatten a
    # proper noun further along the phrase too.
    if phrase.first == 'A'
      assign rest = phrase | remove_first: 'A ' | remove_first: 'An '
      if phrase contains 'An '
        assign phrase = 'an ' | append: rest
      else
        assign phrase = 'a ' | append: rest
      endif
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if phrase != blank -%}
  {%- if bare -%}
    {{- phrase -}}
  {%- else -%}
    {{- 'products.product.drinks_like' | t: phrase: phrase -}}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
